 Points about Collection (Interface)
 -------------------------------------------
-Collection is an interface in Java.
-It represents a group of objects that can be handled together.
-Part of the java.util package.
-Provides basic methods like add(), remove(), size(), clear(), and iterator().
-Implemented by classes like ArrayList, HashSet, LinkedList.
- actual objects stored in memory.
-Cannot be instantiated that mean can not create object directly;
 must use an implementing class to create object.
-Supports generics for type safety.
Example:
Collection<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("Apple");
list.add("Banana");
========================================================================
🔹 Points about Collections (Utility Class)
---------------------------------------------------------------
-Collections is a final class in Java.
-It provides static helper methods to operate on collections.
-In Collections class, all methods are static →
 we don’t need to create a Collections object.
 -These methods help you work with collection objects easily.
 Examples of things we can do:
 Sort a list → Collections.sort(list)
 Reverse a list → Collections.reverse(list)
 Shuffle a list → Collections.shuffle(list)
-Part of the java.util package.
-Common methods include sort(), reverse(), shuffle(), binarySearch(), max(), and min().
-Cannot be extended; all methods are static.
-Does not store objects itself; works on existing collections.
-Cannot be instantiated; methods are called statically.
-Works with any collection type (List, Set, etc.).
Example:
ArrayList<String> fruits = new ArrayList<>();
fruits.add("Banana");
fruits.add("Apple");
fruits.add("Mango");

Collections.sort(fruits);   // Sorts the list
Collections.reverse(fruits); // Reverses the list

🔹 Quick Spoken Tip for Interviews
Collection → “It’s an interface to store and handle groups of objects.”
Collections → “It’s a utility class that provides static methods
 to manipulate collections like sort, reverse, shuffle.”